{
 "title": "Lambda-Calculus and Combinators: An Introduction",
 "byline": "J. Roger Hindley and Jonathan P. Seldin \u00b7 Cambridge University Press, 2008",
 "note": "\u201cFurther reading\u201d notes, the appendix on care of combinators, and answers to exercises are not broken out. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "The standard introduction to the two classic formalisms of computation by substitution, developed side by side. It defines \u03bb-terms and combinatory logic, \u03b2- and weak reduction, fixed points and B\u00f6hm\u2019s theorem, shows both represent exactly the recursive functions and that their equality is undecidable, and treats extensionality and the precise correspondence between \u03bb and CL. The second half is on types: Church-style and Curry-style simple typing, subject reduction, principal types, propositions-as-types, and generalized (dependent, pure) type systems; then models: combinatory algebras, \u03bb-models and Scott\u2019s D\u221e. Appendices prove confluence and strong normalization.",
